.TH cadaver 1 "August 2026" "cadaver-windows" "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
cadaver \- command\-line WebDAV client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cadaver
[\fB\-t\fR] [\fB\-r\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-p\fR \fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]]
[\fB\-c\fR \fIwhat\fR] [\fB\-j\fR] [\fB\-T\fR[\fB=\fIfile\fR]] [\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-V\fR] [\fB\-h\fR] \fIURL\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cadaver
supports file upload, download, on-screen display, in-place editing,
namespace operations (move and copy), collection creation and deletion,
property manipulation and resource locking.

Its operation is similar to the standard BSD
.BR ftp (1)
client and the Samba Project's
.BR smbclient (1).
A user familiar with those tools should be quite comfortable with
cadaver.

.B cadaver
connects to
.IR URL ,
which must be an absolute URI using the
.B http:
or
.B https:
scheme and must name a collection that already exists, and then reads
commands until end of input or
.BR quit .
Credentials must not appear in the URL; see
.B THE .netrc FILE
below.

This is the Windows port, from
.UR https://github.com/Azathothas/cadaver-windows
.UE .
.SH OPTIONS
.IP "\-t, \-\-tolerant"
Allow
.B cd
and
.B open
into a collection that does not advertise WebDAV class 1 support; use
this if the server or proxy has WebDAV compliance problems.
.IP "\-r, \-\-rcfile=\fIfile\fR"
Read commands from
.I file
rather than from the default rcfile.
.B "cadaver \-\-help"
prints the path of the default.
.IP "\-p, \-\-proxy=\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]"
Connect through the proxy server
.I host
on the optional
.IR port .
.IP "\-c, \-\-clobber=\fIwhat\fR"
What
.B get
does when the local file already exists:
.B ask
for another name, which is the default,
.B yes
to overwrite it, or
.B no
to leave it and fail. Asking cannot work in a script, where the prompt
reads end of input.
.IP "\-j, \-\-json"
Write one JSON object describing the session to standard output, and
nothing else. Every command appears in it with its arguments, the
request target, whether it worked, how long it took, the reason and the
HTTP method and status when it did not, and everything it printed. The
shape is documented in
.I AGENTS.md
in the distribution.
.B cat ,
.B less
and
.B edit
are refused under
.BR \-\-json ,
because each writes a byte stream to standard output or runs a program
that might.
.IP "\-T, \-\-trace[=\fIfile\fR]"
Dump every request and response to
.IR file ,
tagged with the command that issued it. Without a file the trace goes
to standard error;
.B \-
means standard output, which
.B \-\-json
then refuses to share. Message bodies are included, except the body of
a transfer, which is the resource itself.
.IP "\-v, \-\-verbose"
Widen the trace to everything neon reports, including socket, TLS, XML
parser and authentication detail. It does not include the credentials
in the clear;
.B "set debug cleartext"
is how to ask for those.
.IP "\-V, \-\-version"
Display version information and exit 0.
.IP "\-h, \-\-help"
Display the option list and the default rcfile path, and exit 0.
.SH COMMAND REFERENCE
.B help
lists the commands and
.B "help \fIcommand\fR"
describes one.
.SS Remote files
.IP "ls [path]"
List contents of the current, or another, collection.
.IP "cd path"
Change to the specified collection.
.B "cd \-"
returns to the previous one.
.IP "pwd"
Display the name of the current collection.
.IP "put local [remote]"
Upload a local file.
.IP "get remote [local]"
Download a remote resource. If the local file already exists, cadaver
asks for another name.
.IP "resumeget remote [local]"
Resume a download into an existing local file, using a
.B Range
request from its current size.
.IP "mget remote..."
Download many remote resources.
.IP "mput local..."
Upload many local files.
.IP "cat remote..."
Write remote resources to standard output, unmodified.
.IP "less remote..."
Display remote resources through the pager.
.IP "head remote"
Show the status and headers of a
.B HEAD
request.
.IP "edit resource"
Lock the resource, download it to a temporary file, run the editor,
upload the result with
.BR If-Match ,
and unlock it.
.IP "mkcol remote..."
Create remote collections.
.IP "delete remote..."
Delete non-collection resources.
.IP "rmcol remote..."
Delete remote collections and all their contents.
.IP "copy source... dest"
Copy resources from source to dest.
.IP "move source... dest"
Move resources from source to dest.
.IP "rename source dest"
Move source to dest with
.BR "Overwrite: F" ,
regardless of the
.B overwrite
option.
.SS Locking
.IP "lock resource"
Lock the given resource.
.IP "unlock resource"
Unlock the given resource.
.IP "discover resource"
Display lock information for the resource.
.IP "steal resource"
Take over the lock tokens held on the resource.
.IP "showlocks"
Display the locks this session holds.
.SS Properties
.IP "propnames res"
Names of the properties defined on the resource.
.IP "propget res [propname]"
Retrieve one property, or all of them.
.IP "propset res propname value"
Set a property on the resource.
.IP "propdel res propname"
Remove a property from the resource.
.IP "chexec [+|\-] remote"
Change the Apache
.B executable
property of the resource.
.SS Searching and versioning
.IP "search query"
DASL search within the current collection. See the
.B searchdepth
and
.B searchorder
options.
.IP "version res"
Place the resource under version control.
.IP "checkin res, checkout res, uncheckout res"
DeltaV check-in, check-out and cancel.
.IP "history res"
Show the version history of the resource.
.IP "label res [add|set|remove] name"
Set, change or remove a label on the resource.
.SS Local and session
.IP "lcd [directory]"
Change the local working directory.
.IP "lls [\-l] [directory]"
List the local directory, in the layout
.B ls
uses.
.IP "lpwd"
Print the local working directory.
.IP "open URL"
Open a connection to the given URL.
.IP "close"
Close the current connection.
.IP "logout"
Forget the credentials for the current authentication session.
.IP "set [option] [value]"
Set an option, or display all of them.
.IP "unset option"
Clear an option.
.IP "describe option"
Explain what an option does.
.IP "echo word..."
Print the arguments.
.IP "about"
Display version information.
.IP "help [command]"
Display the command list, or help for one command.
.IP "quit"
Exit.
.SS Aliases
.BR rm ", " mkdir ", " mv ", " cp " and " more
are aliases for
.BR delete ", " mkcol ", " move ", " copy " and " less ;
.BR exit " and " bye
are aliases for
.BR quit .
.SH WILDCARDS
The commands that take file names expand
.BR * ", " ? ", " [abc] ", " [a\-z] ", " [!abc]
and POSIX character classes such as
.BR [[:digit:]] ,
locally for
.B mput
and against the server for the rest. A remote expansion costs one
.B PROPFIND
per collection it has to look inside, and can be interrupted with
Ctrl\-C.

Matching against the local file system is case-insensitive on Windows
and case-sensitive elsewhere; matching against a server is always
case-sensitive. A pattern that matches nothing is passed through
unexpanded, as a shell does.
.SH QUOTING
A command line is split on whitespace.
.B #
starts a comment, and single or double quotes group an argument that
contains spaces.

On Windows a backslash is an ordinary character, because it is the path
separator:
.B "lcd C:\eUsers\eme"
means what it looks like. Write a name containing a space or a
.B #
in quotes. Everywhere else a backslash quotes the character after it,
as a shell does.
.SH OPTIONS SET WITH set
.B set
with no argument lists the current values.
.IP "tolerant"
Tolerate collections that do not advertise WebDAV.
.IP "overwrite"
Whether
.B copy
and
.B move
overwrite the destination.
.IP "expect100"
Use the
.B "Expect: 100\-continue"
header on uploads.
.IP "utf8"
Treat local names as UTF\-8 rather than converting them.
.IP "quiet"
Whether to print connection progress.
.IP "keepalive"
Use persistent connections.
.IP "clobber"
What
.B get
does when the local file already exists:
.BR ask ", " yes " or " no .
.IP "editor, pager"
Programs to run for
.B edit
and
.BR less .
.IP "namespace"
The XML namespace used by
.B propget
and
.BR propset .
.IP "lockowner, lockscope, lockdepth, lockstore"
Locking behaviour.
.IP "client-cert, client-cert-uri"
A PKCS#12 client certificate, or a URI naming one, for TLS.
.IP "proxy, proxy-port, systemproxy"
Proxy configuration.
.IP "searchall, searchdepth, searchorder, searchdorder"
DASL search behaviour.
.IP "debug"
Protocol tracing.
.B \-\-trace
and
.B \-\-verbose
cover the same ground and tag it with the command that caused it, so
reach for those first; this is for turning one keyword on part way
through a session, and is the only way to ask for the credentials in
the clear. It writes to wherever
.B \-\-trace
was pointed, or to standard error. The value is a
comma-separated list of
.BR http ", " httpbody ", " xml ", " xmlparse ", " socket ", " ssl ", "
.BR httpauth ", " locks ", " files " and " cleartext .
.B cleartext
also shows credentials.
.SH THE .netrc FILE
The file
.I .netrc
in the home directory may be used to log in automatically to a server
requiring authentication. The following tokens, separated by spaces,
tabs or newlines, may be used:

.B machine
.I host
.RS
Identify a remote machine
.I host
which is compared with the hostname given on the command line or as an
argument to the
.B open
command.
Any subsequent tokens up to the end of file or the next
.B machine
or
.B default
token are associated with this entry.
.RE

.B default
.RS
Equivalent to the
.B machine
token but matching any hostname. It is used only when no
.B machine
entry matches, wherever in the file it appears.
.RE

.B login
.I username
.RS
The username to use when logging in to the remote machine.
.RE

.B password
.I string
.br
.B passwd
.I string
.RS
The password to use when logging in to the remote machine.
.RE

Any other tokens, as described in
.BR ftp (1),
are ignored. An entry that supplies a
.B login
and no
.B password
is used for the login, and only the password is asked for.

A quote character quotes only where it opens a value, which is how a
value containing a space is written; inside one it is an ordinary
character. There is no escape, so a value that has to begin with a
quote character cannot be spelled.

The host name is compared without regard to case.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.IP 0
Every command succeeded, or
.B \-\-help
or
.B \-\-version
was asked for.
.IP "1\-125"
That many commands failed. The count is capped at 125 so that it cannot
be read as a shell's signalled exit.
.IP 2
The command line could not be understood, and nothing was executed. A
usage error and two failed commands share this status; nothing ran in
the first case, which is what tells them apart.
.IP "128+\fIn\fR"
The session was ended by signal
.IR n .
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP "cadaver http://myserver.example.com/"
Connect to myserver.example.com and open the root collection.
.IP "cadaver http://zope.example.com:8022/Users/fred/"
Connect to zope.example.com on port 8022 and open the collection
.IR /Users/fred/ .
.IP "cadaver https://secure.example.com/"
Connect over TLS.
.IP "cadaver \-t https://dav.example.com/share/"
Connect to a server that does not advertise WebDAV class 1 support.
.IP "cadaver \-r session.txt https://dav.example.com/uploads/ < /dev/null"
Run the commands in
.I session.txt
and exit.
.SH FILES
.IP ".cadaverrc"
Commands run at startup, in the home directory. On Windows that is
.I %USERPROFILE%
unless
.B $HOME
is set;
.B "cadaver \-\-help"
prints the path. Overridden by
.BR \-\-rcfile .
.IP ".netrc"
Login information used by the auto-login process, in the same
directory. See
.B THE .netrc FILE
above.
.IP ".cadaver-locks"
The lock store, in the same directory.
